# Pepper runs from soap: order matters at the water surface
#
# A safe observation activity with water, ground pepper and a tiny amount of
# dish soap. Do not taste lab materials, and wash the dishes afterwards.
register lv1

# First make the familiar pepper-covered water surface.
add v1 water 100mL
add v1 Pfeffer 0.08g
inspect v1

# Set up a control with the same materials in the opposite order. Once soap is
# already spread across the water there is no new local gradient when pepper
# arrives, so the model must not invent the sudden darting event.
new beaker
add v2 water 100mL
add v2 Spülmittel 1mL
add v2 Pfeffer 0.08g

# Now touch the pepper-first surface with dish soap. The recipe-declared dose
# computes the central clearing; the resulting SurfaceSpread event, retained
# scene state and animation all come from that one engine transition.
add v1 Spülmittel 1mL
inspect v1
inspect v2

# Try less soap in a fresh vessel and predict how wide the clearing will be.
# This is a bounded classroom model, not a molecular surface-tension or CFD
# calculation, and repeating the effect needs fresh water and pepper.
